; EXERCISE 1: INJECTIVE, SURJECTIVE, BIJECTIVE
;
; A, B, C are sets, f and g are maps (theory/maps.kurt lists the symbols and the rules). Replace
; each `todo` by a proof; don't change the `show` lines. Check with
; `kurt 1-injective-surjective.kurt`.
;
; How to use a definition: `injective(f, A)` and `∀ x ∈ A ∀ y ∈ A (f x = f y ⇒ x = y)` follow
; from each other in one step (by "injective"). So to prove `injective(f, A)`, prove the
; ∀ statement (with `let x ∈ A`, lesson 08 of the tutorial), and write `injective(f, A)` after
; the block. To use `injective(g, B)`, write its ∀ statement as a step, and instantiate it one ∀
; at a time: first `∀ v ∈ B (g a = g v ⇒ a = v)`, then `g a = g b ⇒ a = b`.
;
; Kurt does one step at a time: from a premise `P ∧ Q`, write `P` and `Q` as steps of their own
; before you use them.

load theory/maps
const A, B, C, f, g
set A, B, C, f, g        ; sets: a fact `f : A → B` gives no sort

; (a) The identity: a map with f x = x for each x ∈ A is a bijection from A to A.
;     Hint: prove `injective(f, A)` and `surjective(f, A, A)` one after the other. For the first,
;     `let x ∈ A`, then `let y ∈ A`, then `assume f x = f y`.
show ∀ x ∈ A (f x = x) ⇒ bijective(f, A, A)
proof
    todo
qed

; (b) The composition of two injective maps is injective.
;     Hint: `(g ∘ f) x = g (f x)` by "comp" (it needs `f : A → B` as a step), and `f x ∈ B` by
;     "map".
show f : A → B ∧ g : B → C ∧ injective(f, A) ∧ injective(g, B) ⇒ injective(g ∘ f, A)    "comp-injective"
proof
    todo
qed

; (c) The composition of two surjective maps is surjective.
;     Hint: for z ∈ C, there is a y ∈ B with g y = z: `pick y with y ∈ B ∧ g y = z` (lesson 09).
;     Then pick an x for y. Inside `pick x ...`, x is a constant, so write an ∃ with another
;     name for its variable: `∃ u ∈ A ((g ∘ f) u = z)`. A `pick` block passes only its last
;     line on.
show f : A → B ∧ surjective(f, A, B) ∧ surjective(g, B, C) ⇒ surjective(g ∘ f, A, C)    "comp-surjective"
proof
    todo
qed

; (d) The composition of two bijective maps is bijective.
;     Hint: (b) and (c) are proven rules now, with the labels "comp-injective" and
;     "comp-surjective": Kurt uses them like the rules of the scene.
show f : A → B ∧ g : B → C ∧ bijective(f, A, B) ∧ bijective(g, B, C) ⇒ bijective(g ∘ f, A, C)
proof
    todo
qed
